# Pilot measurement record — {{date_long}}

Dodam is a programming language built so that AI writes it correctly and safely, and it is still in its design phase. Before the design is fixed, we first measure how well AI writes in existing languages. Those numbers become the bar Dodam must beat. This document records the pilot: the first time that measurement was run from start to end. It says what was run and how, what came out, what one run used, and how those numbers were used to judge the next measurement and the {{row_month}} schedule.

Every number in this document comes from two data files kept in the repository with it. One holds only the measured values copied from the run records; the other holds the estimates computed from them, with their assumptions. If a number in the document differs from the data, an automatic check fails.

## At a glance

- One sign-up task was run once in each of {{n_runs}} existing languages, {{n_runs}} runs in all. All {{n_ok}} ran to the end.
- {{n_full}} languages got the full score ({{full_reward}}) on both steps. C++ passed every functional check but failed one security check, so it scored {{no_reward}} on both steps.
- In one run the AI worked for {{agent_seconds_mean}} seconds on average, and the run took {{wall_seconds_mean}} seconds from start to end on average.
- This pilot could not settle what percent of the subscription limits one run uses. The weekly utilization did not move by its display step ({{weekly.display_step_points}} percentage point) over {{weekly.later_runs}} runs, and the tool did not report the {{five_hour.guard_window_hours}}-hour limit. Only an upper bound is known.
- The login token was never seen on a command line (the process table was read {{safety.readings}} times), it was not found in any file the runs left, and no container was left.
- The first baseline measurement of {{plan.runs}} runs takes {{x1.nights}} nights on this machine, running at night only, if every run is like the ones measured here. Larger models and other tasks have not been measured.
- The pilot finished earlier than planned. The dates of the {{row_month}} schedule are not changed, because the number that would justify moving them earlier or later (how long a larger model works) has not been measured yet.

## What was run and how

- **Date**: {{date_long}} (UTC).
- **AI**: Claude Code {{cli_version}}, Anthropic's official command-line tool. It was pinned to that version unmodified, and the run records show the same version. No model was chosen, so the tool's default, {{model}}, was used. It ran on a subscription login.
- **Task**: a sign-up and login service ({{task}}). An HTTP API where people sign up, log in and read their own account. It has {{n_steps}} steps: the first builds it from nothing, the second adds a changed requirement (logging out and editing the name).
- **Languages**: Python, TypeScript, Java, C#, Go, Rust and C++, once each.
- **Isolation**: the AI wrote its code inside a container for each language, not on this machine. The result was judged with hidden tests the AI cannot see, in another container with no network and no credentials.
- **Nothing was emulated**: a real model wrote the code on a real subscription account, and the real judge judged it.
- **Order**: the runs came in {{n_batches}} batches. Python ran first, TypeScript about {{weekly.hours_between_first_and_later_runs}} hours later, and the other languages right after it. The runner was changed in between.
- **When the runner stops by itself**: it starts no new run once the weekly utilization reaches {{weekly.runner_stops_at_percent}}%, or once the agents it ran have worked {{five_hour.guard_limit_minutes}} minutes within {{five_hour.guard_window_hours}} hours. One run may take at most {{time_limit_hours}} hours. Nothing was stopped this time.

