### The verdict

- **The pilot**: finished on {{date_long}}. Its mark of being done, "we know what percent of the subscription limit one run uses", is half met. Time and tokens are measured; for the limits only an upper bound is known. The other half is met by the one-night measurement described above.
- **The baseline measurement ({{row_start_long}} to {{row_end_long}})**: the dates stay. At the plan's capacity the chance of ending by {{row_end_long}} was {{planning.before.p_baseline_by_row_end}}% with the inputs before the pilot. After the pilot it is {{planning.after_x1.p_baseline_by_row_end}}% if every run is like the measured ones, {{planning.after_x2.p_baseline_by_row_end}}% if the AI working time is {{x2.factor}} times, and {{planning.after_x4.p_baseline_by_row_end}}% if it is {{x4.factor}} times. The middle dates are {{planning.after_x1.b_baseline.p50}}, {{planning.after_x2.b_baseline.p50}} and {{planning.after_x4.b_baseline.p50}} in that order. The end of the row as it stands lies between "as measured" and "{{x2.factor}} times". Which of them holds is known only once a larger model is measured, so for now there is no ground to move the dates earlier and none to move them later. The day it starts depends on the {{plan.tasks}} tasks and on the measuring tool being complete, and the pilot did not change the amount of that work.
- **The direction-fixing gate ({{gate_long}})**: the date stays. The chance of reaching it by that day was {{planning.before.p_gate_by_its_date}}% with the inputs before the pilot, and after the pilot it is {{planning.after_x1.p_gate_by_its_date}}%, {{planning.after_x2.p_gate_by_its_date}}% and {{planning.after_x4.p_gate_by_its_date}}% in the same order.
- **The time freed by finishing early**: {{pilot_days_before_end}} days remain until the day the pilot was to end. They go to the two pieces of work that decide when the baseline measurement starts (the {{plan.tasks}} tasks and completing the measuring tool) and to the one-night measurement described above. Once its numbers are in, they say which row of the table holds, and the dates are judged again then.
- **About the table for one Claude account**: under that assumption the chance that the baseline measurement ends by {{row_end_long}} was {{claude_only.before.p_baseline_by_row_end}}% before the pilot and is {{claude_only.after_x1.p_baseline_by_row_end}}% after it as measured, and the middle date moves earlier from {{claude_only.before.b_baseline.p50}} to {{claude_only.after_x1.b_baseline.p50}}. The gap to the plan's capacity does not come from the pilot; it comes from the capacity assumption. The pilot itself, on the other hand, was finished with one Claude account {{pilot_days_early}} days earlier than planned. This pilot could not confirm whether about {{claude_only.days_a_week}} working days a week is right, so this table is not used as a ground for changing dates; it is written down to show how far the schedule slips if that assumption holds.
